  <h3>Major Shock Events Timeline</h3>
  <div class="event-list">
    <div class="event-item">
      <div class="event-date">Oct 1973</div>
      <div class="event-body">
        <strong>Arab Oil Embargo (OPEC Shock I)</strong>
        <p>OPEC Arab members embargo oil exports to US, Netherlands, and others supporting Israel in the Yom Kippur War. Price jumps from $4.10 (Dec 1973) to $13.00/bbl in January 1974 — a +217% single-month move, the largest percentage gain in the dataset.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="event-item">
      <div class="event-date">Jan 1979</div>
      <div class="event-body">
        <strong>Iranian Revolution (OPEC Shock II)</strong>
        <p>Iranian oil production collapses, cutting ~4 mb/d from global supply. Prices surge from $14.50 (Dec 1978) to $40.75/bbl (Nov 1979) — a +181% increase over 11 months. The Iran-Iraq War (1980) sustains elevated prices through 1981.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="event-item">
      <div class="event-date">Jan 1986</div>
      <div class="event-body">
        <strong>OPEC Price Collapse</strong>
        <p>Saudi Arabia abandons the swing-producer role and increases output aggressively to defend market share. Prices crater from $26.50 (Dec 1985) to $9.62/bbl (Jul 1986) — a -64% collapse in 7 months. This defines the longest sustained low-price trough in modern oil history.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="event-item">
      <div class="event-date">Aug 1990</div>
      <div class="event-body">
        <strong>Gulf War Price Spike</strong>
        <p>Iraq's invasion of Kuwait triggers a supply shock. Prices jump from $17.17 (Jul 1990) to $34.50/bbl (Oct 1990) — a +101% move in 3 months. The spike reverses rapidly after the US-led coalition liberates Kuwait in early 1991.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="event-item">
      <div class="event-date">Jul 2008</div>
      <div class="event-body">
        <strong>Global Financial Crisis Peak</strong>
        <p>Prices reach the dataset all-time high of $132.83/bbl in July 2008, driven by record demand growth and speculative positioning. The GFC then destroys demand: prices plunge -68.9% to $41.34/bbl by December 2008 — a -$91.49 collapse in just 5 months, the largest absolute decline in the dataset.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="event-item">
      <div class="event-date">Mar 2020</div>
      <div class="event-body">
        <strong>COVID-19 Pandemic Collapse</strong>
        <p>Global lockdowns eliminate aviation and road transport demand. Compounded by a Saudi-Russia price war, prices crash from $61.63 (Jan 2020) to $21.04/bbl (Apr 2020) — a -65.9% decline in 3 months. The year-over-year decline of -69.3% in April 2020 is the largest YoY loss in the dataset.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="event-item">
      <div class="event-date">Feb 2022</div>
      <div class="event-body">
        <strong>Russia-Ukraine War</strong>
        <p>Russia's full-scale invasion of Ukraine triggers sanctions and redirection of energy flows. Prices spike from $83.92 (Jan 2022) to $116.80/bbl (Jun 2022) — a +39.2% increase in 5 months — before retreating as the market adjusts to rerouted Russian oil.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="callout danger">
    <strong>Largest Absolute Monthly Loss:</strong> October 2008 recorded the single largest absolute month-over-month decline: <strong>-$26.96/bbl</strong> (from $99.66 to $72.69), as the full impact of Lehman Brothers' collapse hit energy markets. Over the 5-month window July–December 2008, prices fell a cumulative <strong>-$91.49/bbl</strong> (-68.9%).
  </div>
</div>

