  <h3>Key Structural Observations for Forward Analysis</h3>

  <div class="event-list">
    <div class="event-item">
      <div class="event-date" style="background: var(--accent);">Regime</div>
      <div class="event-body">
        <strong>A New Price Floor Has Emerged</strong>
        <p>Since 2005, no month has recorded a price below $29.78/bbl — even during the severe COVID shock of April 2020, prices only briefly dipped to $21.04/bbl before recovering. This suggests structural demand growth and OPEC+ coordination have established a higher price floor than prevailed in the 1980s–1990s, when prices regularly fell below $15/bbl.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="event-item">
      <div class="event-date" style="background: var(--accent);">CAGR</div>
      <div class="event-body">
        <strong>Long-Run Growth Rate Remains Positive</strong>
        <p>The 7.93% CAGR over 56 years substantially outpaces most fixed-income benchmarks in nominal terms. However, adjusted for inflation, real returns are considerably lower — the 1970s spike accounts for much of the nominal CAGR, and prices in the 2020s remain below 2011–2014 levels even in nominal terms.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="event-item">
      <div class="event-date" style="background: var(--accent);">Volatility</div>
      <div class="event-body">
        <strong>Volatility Is Structurally Elevated in High-Price Regimes</strong>
        <p>The standard deviation of $30.59 over the full period is dominated by the high-price era (2005–2026). The 2000s and 2010s both exhibited CoV above 30%, compared to just 21% in the 1990s. High price levels appear correlated with greater price instability — consistent with the theory that at elevated levels, demand destruction and supply responses are more powerful.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="event-item">
      <div class="event-date" style="background: var(--accent);">2025–26</div>
      <div class="event-body">
        <strong>2025 Downtrend, 2026 Partial Recovery</strong>
        <p>Monthly prices through 2025 trended downward from $67.65 (Jan) to $57.95 (Dec), reflecting the lagged impact of demand softening and OPEC+ production management challenges. March 2026's jump to $87.84/bbl breaks this trend materially — though with only 3 months of 2026 data available, it is premature to characterize this as a new uptrend.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

