  <div class="callout">
    <strong>Concluding Note:</strong> Over 56 years, crude oil markets have demonstrated that price shocks — both up and down — are not aberrations but structural features. The frequency, magnitude, and duration of shocks varies by underlying demand regime: the post-2003 environment of China-driven growth, OPEC+ coordination, and geopolitical friction has made $60–$100/bbl the new "normal" band. Analysts should weight this elevated regime appropriately when constructing price scenarios for the mid-2020s and beyond, while recognizing that the historical data provides ample precedent for sudden exits — in either direction — from any assumed price band.
